swehouse.c: cusp speed groundwork

Adds nothing that runs. A #define, two comment blocks and the forward
declarations the later steps need.

VERY_SMALL_DENOM is 1e-200 and exists because VERY_SMALL, an angular tolerance
of 1e-10 degrees, is the wrong thing to compare a denominator against. Sums of
squares and products of cosines reach 1e-10 at perfectly ordinary
configurations, and refusing there reports a moving cusp as standing still. A
denominator is unusable only when dividing by it would overflow. Guards that
protect the domain of asin() or sqrt() keep VERY_SMALL, where a tolerance
belongs.

The two comment blocks state the units every speed is in, and where each house
system's cusp speeds come from. Both are worth reading before the rest.

Building this step alone warns nine times, "declared static but never defined":
the declarations are here and the definitions arrive in steps 07 and 08. The
pristine file and the finished series are both warning-free.

Part of an eight-step series. Applied in order, the eight produce a file
byte-identical to the single patch "swehouse.c: compute house cusp speeds
analytically"; take either the series or that patch, not both. Every step
compiles. Steps 01 to 06 also apply to a clean tree on their own, so any one of
them can be taken without the rest; 07 needs 01 to 05, not 06; 08 needs all seven to link. Do not
drop 02 or 04 from the middle: nothing else needs them, so the result still
builds clean and is quietly wrong.

